menuconfig MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER
	bool "Cluster config sync (fleet convergence over a dedicated channel)"
	select CRC
	help
	  Fleet configuration convergence (docs/CLUSTER-SYNC-M4.md): every
	  member holds one replicated document — base/ fleet defaults plus
	  nodes/<id>/ pins, each entry independently HLC-stamped — and
	  anti-entropy digests on portnum PRIVATE_APP (256) over a dedicated
	  secondary channel keep the fleet eventually consistent with no
	  leader and no quorum. The channel PSK bounds replication; the
	  security and network config sections are never gossiped. This is
	  zephyrtastic-private protocol: stock nodes drop the frames before
	  decryption and still flood-relay them.

	  Document persistence across reboot additionally requires
	  MESHTASTIC_SETTINGS; without it the doc is RAM-only and re-converges
	  from the fleet after a reset (the level-triggered backstop makes
	  that merely slower, not wrong).

if MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_CHANNEL_NAME
	string "Channel name the cluster module binds to"
	default "cluster"
	help
	  The module resolves this channel by name at use time; with no
	  matching non-disabled channel it idles (one log line says so).
	  Provision it like any secondary channel.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_DIGEST_PERIOD_SEC
	int "Digest broadcast period (seconds)"
	default 300
	range 30 86400
	help
	  Cadence of the ~30-byte digest broadcast (±12% jitter). Push-on-
	  change is the fast path; this is the level-triggered backstop for
	  nodes that were asleep, out of range, or newly joined.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_PULL_DELAY_MS
	int "Maximum delay before answering a digest mismatch (ms)"
	default 3000
	range 0 60000
	help
	  A digest is a broadcast, so every diverged member hears the same one
	  and would pull from the same sender in the same slot. Each waits a
	  random time up to this before sending its ClusterVectorReq.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_TX_GAP_MS
	int "Spacing between the frames of a multi-frame reply (ms)"
	default 500
	range 0 10000
	help
	  A stamp vector or a burst of entries takes several frames. Spacing
	  them leaves the peer airtime to answer instead of transmitting a
	  whole reply into one contention window.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SERVE_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Minimum interval between reply walks we serve (ms)"
	default 5000
	range 0 600000
	help
	  Serving a stamp vector or a burst of entries is the most expensive
	  thing a peer can ask this node to do: tens of bytes in, most of a
	  document out. Answering one at a time bounds concurrency but not
	  RATE — a peer that waits for each walk to finish before asking again
	  extracts a reply every time, indefinitely. This is the rate bound.

	  It is the refill period of a small token bucket, not a hard floor, so
	  a fleet powering up together (every member pulling at once, all of
	  them legitimate) is absorbed by the burst while sustained abuse is
	  still capped. Far below any legitimate cadence — peers pull once per
	  digest period, which is minutes. 0 disables the limit; only useful
	  in tests.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_PUSH_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Minimum interval between change broadcasts we originate (ms)"
	default 30000
	range 0 3600000
	help
	  A local write (promote, pin, unpin) is broadcast once so the fleet
	  does not wait out a digest period. That is the only frame this
	  module originates uninvited, and local writes have no natural rate:
	  a held-down up-arrow or a config that flaps would otherwise be one
	  flood-relayed broadcast per write.

	  This is the refill period of a small token bucket, so a burst of
	  legitimate writes (setting a node up) all air immediately while the
	  sustained rate stays capped. Over budget a push is DROPPED, not
	  queued — the entry is already in the document and the digest still
	  advertises it, so the cost is one digest period of latency and never
	  correctness. 0 disables the limit; only useful in tests.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SYNC_TIMEOUT_SEC
	int "Give up on an unanswered pull after (seconds)"
	default 60
	range 5 3600
	help
	  Only one anti-entropy exchange runs at a time, so a peer that
	  vanishes mid-walk must not hold the slot forever. Nothing is lost
	  when this fires: the documents still differ, so the next digest
	  starts the walk again.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_MAX_ENTRIES
	int "Cluster document capacity (entries)"
	default 32
	range 12 256
	help
	  base sections + per-node pins the document can hold. 32 covers a
	  five-node fleet pinning everything. ~168 B RAM per entry (PSRAM on
	  boards that have it).

	  The floor is twice the shareable-section allowlist, because that is
	  what a CORE-scope node needs to hold its own claim (base + its own
	  sections) and a node that cannot hold its own claim cannot honestly
	  advertise one. A BUILD_ASSERT in meshtastic_cluster.c ties the two
	  together, so growing the allowlist fails the build rather than
	  shipping a node that is over capacity the moment it boots.

choice
	prompt "Document scope this node advertises"
	default MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SCOPE_AUTO
	help
	  What this node CLAIMS to track (docs/CLUSTER-SYNC-M4.md §6.1). The
	  digest is taken over the claim, not over what happens to be held, so
	  a node too small for the whole fleet narrows its claim instead of
	  advertising a document nobody else has.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SCOPE_AUTO
	bool "auto — claim everything, narrow to CORE under table pressure"
	help
	  Start FULL and fall back to CORE the first time the table cannot
	  hold something this node wanted. The right answer for a board with
	  room, because it is a restore source for the fleet right up until
	  the moment it genuinely cannot be.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SCOPE_CORE
	bool "core — base plus this node's own sections (constrained tier)"
	help
	  For boards that will never hold a whole fleet's document (256 KB
	  parts with no PSRAM). CORE is exactly the closure of effective(me,
	  .), so this costs nothing in what the node RUNS; it only stops the
	  node being able to serve other nodes their entries back.

config MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER_SCOPE_FULL
	bool "full — claim everything, never narrow"
	help
	  Pins the claim at FULL. A table that fills then stops tracking part
	  of the fleet and cannot heal — the pre-agents-xhli.10 condition,
	  reinstated deliberately. Choose it only for a node whose job is to
	  be the fleet's restore source, and watch entry_rx_no_space.

endchoice

endif # MESHTASTIC_CLUSTER
